WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.302
Welcome to this Java tutorial, where you'll

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:06.091
distinguish a stream's pending computation from

00:00:06.091 --> 00:00:08.584
its completed result and create a separate

00:00:08.584 --> 00:00:10.067
editable collection copy.

00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:12.619
You'll trace when processing occurs, when a

00:00:12.619 --> 00:00:15.349
fresh stream is needed, and which list an edit

00:00:15.349 --> 00:00:15.824
changes.

00:00:16.476 --> 00:00:19.526
Software often needs both a generated report and

00:00:19.526 --> 00:00:21.178
an editable working draft.

00:00:21.178 --> 00:00:23.783
Separating those objects lets one feature

00:00:23.783 --> 00:00:26.833
preserve its result while another makes changes.

00:00:26.833 --> 00:00:29.566
It also helps you request a new computation

00:00:29.566 --> 00:00:32.171
without trying to reuse a stream that has

00:00:32.171 --> 00:00:33.251
already finished.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.982
A supply desk starts with a request for map, and

00:00:36.982 --> 00:00:38.780
a kit request arrives later.

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.797
Each collected report entry adds Item, a colon,

00:00:41.797 --> 00:00:43.916
and a space before the item name.

00:00:43.916 --> 00:00:46.998
After collection, the desk adds a pen label only

00:00:46.998 --> 00:00:48.282
to an editable copy.

00:00:48.282 --> 00:00:51.300
Our measurements count entries, not characters.

00:00:51.956 --> 00:00:54.819
We'll compare the report size, the edited copy's

00:00:54.819 --> 00:00:57.086
size, and a fresh count of the source.

00:00:57.086 --> 00:00:59.412
First we'll collect before kit arrives.

00:00:59.412 --> 00:01:02.216
Then you'll predict what changes when that same

00:01:02.216 --> 00:01:04.184
addition moves before collection.

00:01:04.184 --> 00:01:06.928
The report must preserve its completed entries

00:01:06.928 --> 00:01:08.956
while the working copy can change.

00:01:09.633 --> 00:01:12.246
Now that we're in the Workspace, let's open

00:01:12.246 --> 00:01:14.920
Main.java and set up the types that give the

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.593
source, pipeline, and report their different

00:01:17.593 --> 00:01:17.958
roles.

00:01:21.939 --> 00:01:24.899
We'll import ArrayList for stored input and the

00:01:24.899 --> 00:01:27.734
editable copy, List for the collected report,

00:01:27.734 --> 00:01:30.064
and Stream for a pending computation.

00:01:30.064 --> 00:01:32.961
Main is the class containing this program, and

00:01:32.961 --> 00:01:35.607
its main method is where execution starts.

00:01:35.607 --> 00:01:38.189
Our statements belong inside that method.

00:01:52.144 --> 00:01:55.001
The report needs an input and a labeling rule

00:01:55.001 --> 00:01:56.144
before it can run.

00:01:56.144 --> 00:01:59.064
Let's create the source with map, then store a

00:01:59.064 --> 00:02:00.842
mapping pipeline in pending.

00:02:00.842 --> 00:02:03.826
The mapping adds the Item label to each element

00:02:03.826 --> 00:02:05.794
when that element is processed.

00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:25.772
Pending holds a description of the work; no

00:02:25.772 --> 00:02:27.728
report has been collected yet.

00:02:27.728 --> 00:02:30.857
This is lazy evaluation: intermediate operations

00:02:30.857 --> 00:02:33.922
describe processing before a terminal operation

00:02:33.922 --> 00:02:34.574
starts it.

00:02:34.574 --> 00:02:37.312
Creating the stream also does not freeze a

00:02:37.312 --> 00:02:39.268
copied list of source entries.

00:02:39.283 --> 00:02:42.171
For this first worked case, let's collect now,

00:02:42.171 --> 00:02:43.615
then add kit afterward.

00:02:43.615 --> 00:02:46.566
Keeping those two statements separate makes the

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:47.508
timing visible.

00:02:55.782 --> 00:02:58.614
To list is the terminal operation that collects

00:02:58.614 --> 00:02:59.276
the report.

00:02:59.276 --> 00:03:02.048
This ArrayList stream observes its source when

00:03:02.048 --> 00:03:04.940
terminal processing begins, which is called late

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:05.422
binding.

00:03:05.422 --> 00:03:07.229
Here only map is present then.

00:03:07.229 --> 00:03:10.061
The following addition changes the source after

00:03:10.061 --> 00:03:12.712
collection has completed; it does not insert

00:03:12.712 --> 00:03:15.182
another entry into that completed report.

00:03:15.207 --> 00:03:18.279
Let's print the size of the collected report so

00:03:18.279 --> 00:03:20.894
the output records what this computation

00:03:20.894 --> 00:03:21.482
produced.

00:03:26.915 --> 00:03:29.263
Reported measures the completed list.

00:03:29.263 --> 00:03:32.310
We also need a working draft that can accept pen

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:34.531
while the report remains unchanged.

00:03:34.531 --> 00:03:37.514
The to list result is unmodifiable: it supports

00:03:37.514 --> 00:03:40.561
reads but rejects adding, removing, or replacing

00:03:40.561 --> 00:03:41.069
entries.

00:03:41.069 --> 00:03:43.480
We'll give the draft its own ArrayList

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:44.115
structure.

00:03:54.372 --> 00:03:56.896
The constructor copies the report's current

00:03:56.896 --> 00:03:59.303
entries into a new list, and the addition

00:03:59.303 --> 00:04:00.653
targets only that copy.

00:04:00.653 --> 00:04:03.471
This is a shallow copy: the lists initially hold

00:04:03.471 --> 00:04:05.760
references to the same String elements.

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:08.226
The constructor does not create new String

00:04:08.226 --> 00:04:08.695
objects.

00:04:08.695 --> 00:04:11.220
Adding pen changes list membership, without

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:12.922
changing any String in place.

00:04:12.947 --> 00:04:15.971
Let's print the original report and copy sizes

00:04:15.971 --> 00:04:19.126
separately, then request a fresh stream to count

00:04:19.126 --> 00:04:19.849
the source.

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:22.872
A stream is single use, so this new count must

00:04:22.872 --> 00:04:25.765
not reuse pending after its earlier terminal

00:04:25.765 --> 00:04:26.422
operation.

00:04:46.201 --> 00:04:48.999
Count returns long, Java's whole-number type

00:04:48.999 --> 00:04:50.780
with a wider range than int.

00:04:50.780 --> 00:04:52.878
We can print that value directly.

00:04:52.878 --> 00:04:55.931
The report was collected before kit arrived; the

00:04:55.931 --> 00:04:58.538
working copy received pen; the source now

00:04:58.538 --> 00:05:00.001
contains both requests.

00:05:00.001 --> 00:05:02.926
Let's click Run to compare those measurements.

00:05:08.547 --> 00:05:11.181
Reported and Original result are both one.

00:05:11.181 --> 00:05:14.191
The completed report still contains only the map

00:05:14.191 --> 00:05:14.568
label.

00:05:14.568 --> 00:05:17.265
The editable copy grows to two after pen is

00:05:17.265 --> 00:05:17.641
added.

00:05:17.641 --> 00:05:20.651
Fresh count is two because the new stream counts

00:05:20.651 --> 00:05:22.909
the source after kit has been added.

00:05:22.909 --> 00:05:25.543
Reading the report again did not rerun the

00:05:25.543 --> 00:05:26.672
consumed pipeline.

00:05:26.738 --> 00:05:29.349
Now let's move the kit addition before the

00:05:29.349 --> 00:05:30.593
report is collected.

00:05:30.593 --> 00:05:33.266
We'll replace just these two lines in their

00:05:33.266 --> 00:05:34.199
opposite order.

00:05:34.199 --> 00:05:37.184
The source values, mapping, copy edit, and print

00:05:37.184 --> 00:05:38.738
statements stay the same.

00:05:47.381 --> 00:05:49.768
Before running, predict all four lines.

00:05:49.768 --> 00:05:52.215
Which source entries exist when terminal

00:05:52.215 --> 00:05:53.561
processing starts now?

00:05:53.561 --> 00:05:56.437
Which list receives pen, and which entries does

00:05:56.437 --> 00:05:57.906
the fresh count measure?

00:05:57.906 --> 00:06:00.660
Use those three object roles to explain which

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.006
numbers should change.

00:06:10.087 --> 00:06:12.598
Let's click Run and compare your timing

00:06:12.598 --> 00:06:14.787
prediction with the actual output.

00:06:20.260 --> 00:06:23.326
Reported and Original result are now two because

00:06:23.326 --> 00:06:26.265
map and kit are both present before collection

00:06:26.265 --> 00:06:26.712
begins.

00:06:26.712 --> 00:06:29.714
The copy starts with those two labels and grows

00:06:29.714 --> 00:06:31.439
to three when pen is added.

00:06:31.439 --> 00:06:34.377
Fresh count stays two: it measures the source,

00:06:34.377 --> 00:06:37.060
which never received the copy's pen entry.

00:06:37.111 --> 00:06:39.485
You separated a pending computation, a completed

00:06:39.485 --> 00:06:41.315
report, and an editable working list.

00:06:41.315 --> 00:06:43.294
Late binding explained the input seen at

00:06:43.294 --> 00:06:45.372
collection; a fresh stream enabled another

00:06:45.372 --> 00:06:47.696
computation; and a shallow collection copy gave

00:06:47.696 --> 00:06:49.230
edits their own list structure.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.406
For a transfer task, describe a report whose

00:06:51.406 --> 00:06:53.088
input can change after generation.

00:06:53.088 --> 00:06:55.462
Explain when you would read the existing report,

00:06:55.462 --> 00:06:57.292
copy it for editing, or request a new

00:06:57.292 --> 00:06:57.886
computation.
